    <section class="section section-compact" id="questions" aria-labelledby="questions-title">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 id="questions-title" class="h2">Questions.</h2>
        <div class="faq faq-stack">
          <div class="faq-col">
            <details>
              <summary>What does it cost?</summary>
              <p>Nothing during early access. Pricing isn't set yet, and invites go out from the waitlist.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
              <summary>How is it different from Slack?</summary>
              <p>Channels, threads and DMs work the way you'd expect. The difference is what happens afterwards: <a href="#memory">decisions become memos</a>, <a href="#board">conversations become cards</a>, and <a href="#agents">your own agent</a> can join in.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
              <summary>Can the AI read everything?</summary>
              <p>Not what's in a <a href="#privacy">sealed scratchpad</a>. Everywhere else, a memo is only shown to people who can open the conversation it came from.</p>
            </details>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-col">
            <details id="your-data">
              <summary>Can I take my data with me?</summary>
              <p>There's no export button yet. You can edit or delete your own messages, and edit or archive the memos Threa keeps. Messages and memos can be read through the <a href="/developers">public API and the CLI</a>, so a script can copy them out. The code is MIT licensed, so you can also run your own copy from the same repo we deploy.</p>
              <p>A workspace's data is kept until the workspace is deleted. Deleting one from inside Threa isn't built yet.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
              <summary>Is there a phone app?</summary>
              <p>Threa is an installable web app that works offline. <a href="#install">Install it from your browser</a> on iPhone, Android or a computer.</p>
            </details>
            <details>
              <summary>Who builds it?</summary>
              <p>One developer, Kristoffer Remback, in the open. <a href="/about">Why he's building it</a>.</p>
            </details>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

